---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/intermediate/sampling-and-survey-design">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is the statistics track's gatekeeping department: every mean, interval and
    p-value downstream is computed from a sample, and this page decides whether that sample
    deserves the arithmetic. <strong>Describing Data</strong> taught you what to do with
    collected numbers; <strong>Probability Foundations</strong> supplies the randomness
    that lets a drawn sample predict its population at all. Nothing else is assumed — the
    vocabulary (population, frame, stratum) is built here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define population, sample, census and sampling frame — and locate where each bias enters;</li>
    <li>execute simple random, systematic, stratified and quota sampling, including the random-number-table procedure;</li>
    <li>diagnose the recurring biases — selection, non-response, volunteer, question order — and say why sample size never fixes them;</li>
    <li>write survey questions that measure answers rather than manufacture them;</li>
    <li>classify data as qualitative or quantitative, discrete or continuous, and say which analyses each classification permits.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The page's through-line: representativeness is a property of the <em>method</em>, never
    of the count.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You almost never get to measure everyone — you <strong>sample</strong>, and statistics tells
    you exactly how much the sample can be trusted. The central surprise: a well-drawn sample
    of ~1,000 people predicts a nation of 300 million within ±3 points, regardless of population
    size. Precision depends on <strong>n, not on N</strong> — and on the sample being truly
    random. That claim deserves unpacking, because it is counterintuitive: how can 0.0003% of
    a country speak for it? The answer is that a <em>randomly drawn</em> sample inherits the
    population's proportions in predictable ways, and the mathematics of those fluctuations
    (which the confidence-intervals lesson makes exact) is remarkably well behaved. But the
    emphasis must sit on "randomly drawn": nearly every famous polling disaster is a failure
    of <em>who was asked</em>, not how many. This page covers the vocabulary (population,
    sample, census, sampling frame), the four sampling methods the syllabus requires — with
    the random-number-table procedure worked out — the ways samples go wrong, the craft of
    writing survey questions, and the classification of data that determines everything
    downstream.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the 1/√n law — quadrupling the sample only halves the error bar" widget="sliderLab" config={sampleCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> one slider, but the readouts tell a story. Slide n
    upward from 400 and watch the margin of error fall by the square-root law — each extra
    point of precision costs visibly more interviews than the last. Slide it downward
    instead and the error bar balloons toward the sizes where careless polling lives. The
    panel's lesson is economic as much as statistical: precision has a price curve, and
    design — randomness, stratification, weighting — buys more than raw size ever does.
  </p>

  <p>
    That square root is why polls cost what they cost: halving the margin of error quadruples the
    interviews. It also explains why "we surveyed 10,000 people!" often means little — if the
    10,000 weren't random, no sample size saves you.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a big enough sample fixes a biased one. Bias is a systematic tilt, not noise, and averaging more biased data just averages the tilt more precisely — the Literary Digest's 2.4 million respondents produced one of history's worst polling errors because its frame excluded poorer voters. Sample size multiplies whatever the method already is.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> samples speak for populations at a square-root price, and the
    "randomly drawn" clause is where every famous polling disaster lives. Next: the
    vocabulary that makes the failure precise — population, sample, census, and the frame
    where the first bias enters.
  </p>

  <h2>Population, sample and census</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>population</strong> is everyone (or everything) the question is about — not the
    general public, but precisely the group you want to describe: all voters in a
    country, all oak trees in a wood, every bolt in a production run. The
    <strong>sample</strong> is the part you actually measure. A <strong>census</strong> tries
    to measure the whole population, and its problems teach why sampling wins:
    a census is slow and ruinously expensive; it strains to reach everyone (the
    undercount of young men and renters is a persistent census problem); and total
    counts are not even error-free — more enumerators means more data-entry, more
    inconsistency, more chances to err. Sampling a well-chosen fraction, measured
    carefully by fewer trained people, is often <em>more</em> accurate than a
    sprawling census — the insight that transformed official statistics in the
    1930s–40s. One term does the quiet work in between: the <strong>sampling
    frame</strong> is the actual list you draw from (a school register, a phone
    directory, an electoral roll). The frame is where the first bias enters,
    because the frame and the population are rarely the same thing: the 1936
    <em>Literary Digest</em> polled 2.4 million people from telephone directories
    and car registries during the Depression — a frame that excluded poorer
    voters — and confidently predicted the wrong US president. George Gallup,
    with a far smaller but better-drawn sample, got it right.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> to describe a large population reliably you must sample a large fraction of it. Precision depends on the absolute sample size, not the population — a well-drawn 1,000-person sample carries roughly a 3-point margin of error whether the country holds 3 million people or 300 million, which is why national polls cluster around 1,000 to 2,000 respondents.</p>

  <h2>Sampling methods I: simple random and the random number table</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Simple random sampling</strong> gives every member of the frame an equal chance of
    selection, independently — the statistical ideal, and the procedure is worth
    knowing exactly. <strong>Worked with a random number table:</strong> a school has
    864 students and you need 30. First number the students 001 to 864 (all
    three-digit numbers). Then enter the table of random digits anywhere (close
    your eyes and point — the starting point must itself be arbitrary) and read
    digits in groups of three: 402, 781, 937, 056, 223, 990, 114 … Take 402, 781,
    056, 223, 114; <em>skip</em> 937 and 990 because no student carries those
    numbers (outside the range); skip any repeat (a student drawn twice stays in
    once); and continue until 30 numbers are collected. The same job is done by
    drawing numbered tickets from a well-mixed hat or by a computer's random
    function — the requirement is not the tool but the <strong>equal, known chance</strong>
    for every member, decided by chance rather than by anyone's convenience.
    Note what "random" does <em>not</em> mean: it does not mean haphazard. Asking
    the first 30 students in the corridor is haphazard but badly biased (early
    risers, one year group, one corridor); randomness is a discipline that
    removes human choice precisely because human choice correlates with the
    answer.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a random sample is a haphazard one — asking whoever turns up. Haphazard is exactly what randomness exists to prevent: the first 30 students in a corridor share a timetable, and human choice always correlates with the answer. Random means every member of the frame has a known, calculable chance of selection, decided by chance machinery rather than convenience.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> simple random sampling is the ideal, and the random-number
    table shows it to be a discipline rather than a shrug — every member holding an equal,
    known chance. Next: the three workhorse variants — systematic, stratified and quota —
    and the one that quietly re-imports the bias randomness exists to remove.
  </p>

  <h2>Sampling methods II: systematic, stratified and quota</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Systematic sampling</strong> takes every k-th member of the frame after a
    random start: for 864 students and a sample of 30, choose every 29th
    (864 ÷ 30 ≈ 28.8, round to 29) starting from a student chosen at random
    among the first 29. It is cheap, spreads evenly across the frame, and is
    effectively random <em>unless</em> the list has a repeating pattern aligned
    with the interval — every 29th house on a street where blocks repeat every
    30 houses would be a self-inflicted wound. <strong>Stratified sampling</strong>
    splits the frame into strata that matter to the question (year groups,
    regions, age bands) and samples each in proportion. The arithmetic is the
    exam staple and the intuition is important: a school of 1,200 with 720
    girls and 480 boys, sampled at 100, takes 100 × (720 ÷ 1200) = 60 girls and
    40 boys — guaranteeing the sample mirrors the population's structure
    (then draw randomly <em>within</em> each stratum). Stratification usually
    improves precision when the strata genuinely differ on the measured
    quantity. <strong>Quota sampling</strong> looks similar but swaps chance for
    instruction: interviewers are told to fill quotas ("20 women aged 18–30",
    "10 men over 60") and choose whom to ask until each quota fills. It is
    fast, cheap and needs no sampling frame — which is why market research and
    street interviews use it — but the choice within each quota is
    unconstrained, so it re-imports exactly the bias randomness exists to
    remove (interviewers approach people who look approachable). Quota is
    best understood as stratification without the random step.
  </p>

  <h2>Bias: how samples lie</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bias</strong> is any systematic tendency for the sample to misrepresent the
    population — a tilt, not noise, and averaging more data does not remove it.
    The recurring species:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Selection bias:</strong> the frame or draw favours some members. The <em>Literary Digest</em> case; online polls that whoever clicks can enter; "man on the street" vox pops outside a gym.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-response bias:</strong> the sampled people who decline differ from those who answer — busy people miss surveys; angry or delighted customers fill feedback forms; phone polls reach only the reachable. Modern polls fight this with reweighting and response-rate reporting.</li>
    <li><strong>Volunteer/self-selection bias:</strong> a species of the above — radio phone-ins, review sites — where motivation to answer correlates with the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Question-order and interviewer effects:</strong> earlier questions prime later answers; who asks, and how, shifts results measurably.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The professional response to most of these is <strong>weighting</strong>: after
    collection, adjust the sample so its composition (age, sex, region,
    education) matches known population figures, overweighting the
    under-represented. Weighting rescued election polling from its 2016
    failures (education weighting, after polls missed less-educated
    voters) — but it is a repair, not a substitute for a good draw: no
    weighting can fix a frame that never contained part of the population.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bias is a tilt, not noise — weighting repairs composition, but
    no weighting repairs a frame that never contained part of the population. Next: even a
    perfect draw dies at the questionnaire, where wording either manufactures answers or
    measures them.
  </p>

  <h2>Asking: open and closed questions</h2>
  <p>
    A survey measures answers only as well as the questions elicit them.
    <strong>Closed questions</strong> offer fixed options — good for comparability and
    easy analysis, provided the options are <strong>exhaustive</strong> (include
    "neutral" or "don't know") and <strong>balanced</strong> (both directions, equally
    phrased — not "excellent / good / fair", which is a compliment menu).
    <strong>Open questions</strong> ("What would improve the canteen?") capture the
    unexpected and reveal reasoning, at the cost of slow, judgement-laden
    analysis — surveys typically pair a few open questions among many closed
    ones. The craft's failure catalogue is worth memorising:
    <strong>leading questions</strong> ("Don't you agree the canteen food is
    terrible?"); <strong>loaded wording</strong> ("Should we expand the wasteful
    welfare programme?" — one policy, two adjectives, two different polls);
    <strong>double-barrelled questions</strong> ("Are you satisfied with the price
    and quality of the food?" — which one?); <strong>overlapping or unbalanced
    options</strong> (age bands "0–20, 20–40, 40–60" leave 20 and 40 double-
    counted); and <strong>embarrassment effects</strong>, where socially awkward
    truths (drinking, prejudice) get under-reported unless questions are
    indirect or anonymised. Professionals pilot-test on a small group,
    randomise question order where priming is a risk, and pre-register
    the wording before results exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Classifying data: the vocabulary everything downstream uses</h2>
  <p>
    Before analysis, classify what you collected — the classification decides
    which averages, charts and tests are legal. <strong>Qualitative</strong>
    (categorical) data names a category: eye colour, favourite subject,
    yes/no. <strong>Quantitative</strong> data is numerical, and splits again:
    <strong>discrete</strong> values are countable and separate (number of siblings,
    goals scored — no 2.5 siblings), while <strong>continuous</strong> values lie on
    a scale where any interval contains more values (height, time, temperature
    — measured, not counted). Two refinements matter in practice. Ordinal
    data (satisfaction ratings, finishing positions) is qualitative but
    <em>ordered</em> — you may report a median, not a mean. And measurement
    granularity is a human choice: shoe sizes are discrete because the
    manufacturer says so; feet are continuous. The classification is not
    pedantry: a pie chart of continuous data is a category error, a mean of
    "Strongly disagree = 1 … Strongly agree = 5" codes pretends distance
    between labels that may not exist, and every test the hypothesis lesson
    introduces will begin by asking which kind of data you have.
  </p>

  <h2>The sampling frame decides what you can claim</h2>
  <p>
    One refinement ties the methods together. Every sample is drawn from a
    <strong>frame</strong> — a school register, a customer list, a phone directory —
    and the frame, not the map, defines the population your results describe:
    a poll of a register of landlines describes people with landlines, whatever
    the questionnaire calls them. So the first professional question is not
    "which method?" but "does the frame cover the population?" — and the
    honest report names its frame and its coverage gap. The second
    question is "does the method give everyone in the frame a known,
    non-zero chance?" — the definition of a <strong>probability sample</strong>
    (simple random, systematic with random start, stratified) versus a
    <strong>non-probability</strong> one (quota, convenience, volunteer), whose
    samples can still be useful but whose precision no formula can
    certify. The vocabulary matters because it is how surveys defend
    themselves: the margin of error printed under a poll is a promise
    about probability sampling from a stated frame, and every
    "10,000 readers voted" number in your feed is a volunteer
    sample wearing a poll's clothes.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A school of 1,200 students has 720 girls and 480 boys. A stratified sample of 100 is drawn — how many boys, and why that number?</li>
    <li>The 1936 Literary Digest polled 2.4 million people and predicted the wrong US president. What single word names the failure, and why did the size not help?</li>
    <li>Why does quadrupling a poll's sample from 1,000 to 4,000 shrink its margin of error only from about 3.1 to about 1.55 points?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) 100 × (480 ÷ 1200) = 40 boys — each stratum contributes
    in proportion to its share of the population, so the sample mirrors the population's
    structure, which typically tightens precision when the strata differ on what you
    measure. (2) Selection bias: the frame — telephone directories, car registries, its
    own wealthier readership — systematically excluded poorer voters during the Depression,
    and a biased method only becomes more precisely biased as n grows; Gallup's far
    smaller random sample got it right. (3) The margin of error falls as 1/√n: four times
    the sample gives half the error bar, and ten times the precision needs a hundred times
    the interviews — precision is sold at a square-root price.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Precision depends on the sample size n, not on the population size N.</li>
    <li>Randomness is a discipline — equal, known chances, decided by chance machinery — never haphazard convenience.</li>
    <li>Stratified sampling mirrors the population's structure and usually tightens precision; quota is stratification without the random step.</li>
    <li>Bias is a systematic tilt: averaging more biased data averages the tilt more precisely.</li>
    <li>The frame defines the population you can honestly describe — name it, and name its coverage gap.</li>
    <li>Closed questions must be exhaustive and balanced; leading, loaded and double-barrelled wording manufactures answers.</li>
    <li>Classify before analysing: qualitative or quantitative, discrete or continuous decides which charts, averages and tests are legal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The drawn sample now feeds the rest of the track: <strong>Confidence Intervals</strong>
    turns its size into an error bar, <strong>Hypothesis Testing</strong> weighs its
    evidence against a null, and <strong>Correlation &amp; Regression</strong> asks how two
    of its variables move together — each calculation only as honest as the draw this page
    designed.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cluster sampling and the cost of contact.</strong> When the population is
    spread out, visiting randomly chosen individuals is ruinous; instead sample
    <em>clusters</em> (random schools, then everyone within — or random households,
    then one person each). Clustering trades precision for feasibility, because
    people in a cluster resemble each other; the design effect quantifies the
    loss. Real national surveys are almost always multi-stage: random regions,
    then random neighbourhoods, then addresses, then one respondent — plus
    stratification and weighting. The pure methods of this page are the
    vocabulary those designs are built from.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The paradox of precision.</strong> The 1/√n law has a political economy:
    doubling precision costs four times the fieldwork, so professional polls
    cluster at n ≈ 1,000 (±3 points) — enough for most questions and all
    headlines. The exceptions are instructive: exit polls need speed and
    cluster designs; small subgroups (18–24-year-old men in one region) inherit
    the full sample's smallness, which is why "subgroup findings" from the same
    poll are far less precise than the topline — a reading skill the news-vs-
    opinion lesson's genre literacy should include.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the field is moving.</strong> Falling response rates (telephone
    polls now often reach under 10% of those dialled) are pushing surveys
    toward probability panels, administrative data linkage and — more
    controversially — opt-in web panels corrected by aggressive statistical
    modelling. Whether model-adjusted non-probability samples can match true
    random samples is the live methodological argument; the 2020–2024 US
    polling literature is the running scoreboard.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Groves et al., Survey Methodology; OpenStax, Introductory Statistics (Sampling and Data); Gallup's 1936 account of the Literary Digest failure.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself" widget="quiz" config={sampleQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four items are the page's load-bearing cases in
    miniature: the Literary Digest (representativeness is a property of the method, not the
    count), the stratified-arithmetic exam staple, the anatomy of a fair closed question,
    and the square-root law priced in interviews. Notice how often "big" or "more" appears
    in the wrong answers — sample size as a cure for bad design is precisely the folk
    remedy this page exists to retire.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
